INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti Ransomware public-source investigation batch", "source_url": "https://rewardsforjustice.net/rewards/conti/; https://www.cisecurity.org/insights/blog/the-conti-leaks-a-case-of-cybercrimes-commercialization; https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/2022/03/01/conti-ransomware-group-internal-chats-leaked-over-russia-ukraine-conflict/; https://www.justice.gov/usao-mdtn/conti-ransomware-2", "text": "Benchmark sources report: Rewards for Justice describes Conti, also known as Wizard Spider, as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti, and lists associated location Russia, organization Conti/Wizard Spider, and applications Conti RaaS, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. RFJ says Conti was first detected in 2019, used in more than 1,000 operations, and victimized more than 400 organizations worldwide, over 290 in the U.S.; these are government-site assertions. CIS explains RaaS as developers leasing malware to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, often 70/30, and describes Conti double extortion, ties to TrickBot, internal leaks after the February 2022 Ukraine invasion, business units, distinct roles (Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers), outsourced spamming, separate budgets, and an insider estimate of more than 60 employees in July 2021. CIS reports post-leak dismantling/rebranding into smaller units and later shutdown of leak and negotiation infrastructure. Rapid7 reports the Feb. 27 2022 ContiLeaks disclosure of 14 MB across 393 JSON files, some encrypted and incomplete; chats, screenshots, tools, exploits, design processes, credentials and alleged server details were leaked. Rapid7 assesses the leaker was likely a senior member or disgruntled affiliates, and describes interviews, coding exercises, salary negotiations, product development, phishing, endpoint-evasion testing, a control panel, and well-organized/funded operations; it reports an alleged FSB funding conversation but no independent verification in the text. DOJ identifies defendants Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov in case 3:23-CR-00092, charged in Sept. 2023 with CFAA and wire-fraud conspiracy for roles in the Conti ransomware conspiracy; Oleksii Lytvynenko in case 3:23-CR-00088, charged in Nov. 2025 with conspiracy to deploy Conti and later pleaded guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud in June 2026. DOJ explicitly says defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty. The benchmark search results did not establish any mapping between these named defendants and the RFJ aliases; do not infer one."}
